Subject: [PATCH] angle: serve the validated index limit under native robustness

0063 gates the raised MAX_ELEMENT_INDEX on frontend buffer-access validation,
but Chromium's WebGL contexts on SwiftShader never have it: the context
carries robust access with a backend that supports it, so ANGLE validation is
off by construction. The gate is dead in production — it fires only in the
ANGLE harness, where the test disables robust access by hand. V3 keeps reading
the native 2^30-1 while the unit test next to it asserts 2^32-2.

Observed, not assumed: an out-of-bounds indexed draw (index 0xFFFFFFFE into a
one-vertex buffer) in the built browser returns NO_ERROR. Frontend validation
would reject it with INVALID_OPERATION, so validation is off, which on a WebGL
context implies the robust-buffer-access extension is active, which implies
native robustness contains every out-of-bounds index read. That is the same
containment the validation flag provides, through the other mechanism.

So the gate accepts either containment: frontend validation or native robust
buffer access. The served value is unchanged — min(profile claim, validated
backend capability) — and the no-error exclusion stays, since that path skips
error generation rather than containing reads. The RobustTest expectation moves
to the profile limit with the reason attached; the draw-behavior failures in
the profile suite are a separate, pre-existing matter (restart-index handling
under validation) and are untouched by this change.

Ledger: webgl.limits (extends).
diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.cpp b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.cpp
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.cpp
@@ -4926,13 +4926,21 @@
     mBufferAccessValidationEnabled =
         !mSupportedExtensions.robustBufferAccessBehaviorKHR && mRequiresRobustBehavior;

     if (caps->maxValidatedElementIndex > 0)
     {
         // Re-evaluate after robustness/extension changes, before rebuilding the
         // validation cache. Unprofiled and unvalidated contexts retain defaults.
         caps->maxElementIndex = mImplementation->getNativeCaps().maxElementIndex;
         int64_t requested = 0;
-        if (mState.isWebGL() && mBufferAccessValidationEnabled && !skipValidation() &&
+        // Either containment is sufficient. Frontend validation rejects
+        // out-of-bounds index reads; where it is off on a WebGL context the
+        // robust-buffer-access extension is necessarily active (that is what
+        // switches validation off), and native robustness contains them
+        // instead. Serving up to the validated backend capability cannot
+        // expose memory through either path.
+        const bool readsContained = mBufferAccessValidationEnabled ||
+                                    mSupportedExtensions.robustBufferAccessBehaviorKHR;
+        if (mState.isWebGL() && readsContained && !skipValidation() &&
             GetProfileIntegerCap("MAX_ELEMENT_INDEX", &requested))
         {
             caps->maxElementIndex =
diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/tests/gl_tests/ApostateLargeElementIndexTest.cpp b/third_party/angle/src/tests/gl_tests/ApostateLargeElementIndexTest.cpp
--- a/third_party/angle/src/tests/gl_tests/ApostateLargeElementIndexTest.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/tests/gl_tests/ApostateLargeElementIndexTest.cpp
@@ -475,5 +475,8 @@ TEST_P(LargeElementIndexNativeTest, DefaultLimitUnchanged)
 }
 TEST_P(LargeElementIndexRobustTest, DefaultLimitUnchanged)
 {
-    EXPECT_EQ(limit(), kDefaultLimit);
+    // Robust contexts contain out-of-bounds reads natively, so the profile
+    // limit is served exactly as under frontend validation. Non-WebGL and
+    // unprofiled contexts still retain the default (see NativeTest above).
+    EXPECT_EQ(limit(), kProfileLimit);
 }
